@GeneralistAI brought robotics into the pretraining era. With GEN-1, we’ve been working hard to harness the raw potential of true robot foundation models and bring them into the mastery era.

For us, mastery is about three axes: reliability, speed, and improvisation. Real-world deployment requires many nines of reliability, and we aim for no more slow robots. We want robots that can think on their feet and figure out how to get through tough situations.

In all of the tasks we are showing today, the models have only experienced one single hour of robot data, including pretraining. With a powerful pretraining base, mastery becomes a process of alignment, teaching robots what their task is, and they’ll figure out the rest.

@GeneralistAI: "Introducing GEN-1. Our latest milestone in scaling robot learning. We believe it to be the first general-purpose AI model to master simple physical tasks. 99% success rates, 3x faster speeds, adapts in real time to unexpected scenarios, with only 1 hour of robot data."

Generalist AI has just broken the 'impossible triangle' of speed, reliability, and intelligence in robotics. They’ve significantly advanced their embodied foundation model: GEN-1 hits 'Mastery' on simple physical tasks with a 99% average success rate and the ability to improvise when things go wrong.

For instance, GEN-1 can fold boxes with 200 runs at 99% success in about 12 seconds (down from ~34 seconds). It also excels at packing phones, folding T-shirts, and servicing robot vacuums, achieving hundreds of reps with almost zero failures. The model requires only about one hour of robot-specific data per task, marking a real step toward reliable humanoid robots for factories, warehouses, or homes. Physical AGI is accelerating.

@GeneralistAI: "Introducing GEN-1. Our latest milestone in scaling robot learning. We believe it to be the first general-purpose AI model to master simple physical tasks. 99% success rates, 3x faster speeds, adapts in real time to unexpected scenarios, with only 1 hour of robot data. More..."

"Nobody programmed the robot to recover from mistakes. It just happens for free." Generalist AI’s new GEN-1 model is demonstrating what they call "intelligent improvisation." In a washer task, the robot realizes its grip orientation is wrong and autonomously uses its other arm to nudge the part into place. This isn't a scripted recovery; it’s an emergent capability born from scaling to 500,000 hours of physical data.

@humanoidsdaily: "Is robotics having its 'GPT-3 moment'? 🤖 Generalist AI’s new GEN-1 model is shattering benchmarks: 99% success rate (up from 64%), 3x faster speeds, and only 1 hour of robot data required. From 'data hands' to intelligent improvisation, here is how scaling laws are mastering the physical world."

The linked article discusses how Generalist AI's GEN-1, trained on extensive interaction data, has achieved significant improvements in task performance and speed, showcasing the potential of emergent behaviors in robotics.

Since the release of GEN-1 by @peteflorence and the team at @GeneralistAI, along with DVA by @startupjag and the team at @rhodaai, there’s been a noticeable spike in belief around end-to-end robot policies and large pretrained models. This shift is not about magically solving all problems but rather about how pretraining changes the slope of the problem, allowing for learning priors over entire distributions of visual and physical interactions instead of engineering every edge case explicitly.

While we still need guardrails, task-specific adaptation, and careful evaluation, the burden shifts from manual engineering of corner cases to letting models absorb variability implicitly. This results in reduced commissioning time, as deployments no longer start from scratch for every new scenario. The real advancement is compressing years of edge-case engineering into a pretrained prior, marking a significant step-change for real-world robotics, rather than just another demo cycle.

