Robotics pre-training from scratch has been seen as a controversial idea due to the belief that there is no internet of robotics data. This has led to two conclusions: the need for pretrained model backbones and the necessity to scale robotics data. The first conclusion raises concerns about whether models designed for text, vision, and audio will be suitable for robot actions as a primary modality.

The second conclusion about scaling robotics data is more straightforward, but the execution is complex, as the type and method of scaling significantly impact outcomes. @GeneralistAI has taken a unique approach by avoiding pretrained representations and co-designing novel hardware, data collection, and models from scratch, demonstrating strong conviction and long-term commitment. There are still many open research questions in Physical AI, and the progress made by @peteflorence, @andyzengineer, @coolboi95, and @ColinearDevin in this differentiated direction is commendable.

@peteflorence: "In GEN-1, approximately 99% of the parameters are trained from scratch. Previously, this might be considered wild. For Generalist, it’s a deliberate choice."

Great observations and two quick responses. Chinese companies can certainly have advantages in scaling data operations, but we can (and must) scale fast as well. Large-scale pretraining is only one key component in the picture.

Our position is not that VLMs, World Models, the Internet, or YouTube are not valuable sources; they absolutely are! The contrast is that at @GeneralistAI, we approached the robot foundation model (RFM) problem unencumbered by reliance on these sources. Now that we understand how RFMs should be built, we can integrate all these valuable sources into a first-class native robot foundation model design.

@keerthanpg: "This is an amazing blog post, but the conclusions that fall out of it are quite grim. If pre-training from scratch is required, then robotic capabilities will scale as a function of operations expense - linear with respect to the number of people employed, number of collection hardware, etc. This leaves most American companies in a hard place as China is better positioned to scale operations due to large-scale manufacturing and cheap labor. That we cannot bootstrap off of priors from AI models like VLMs and World Models foreshadows a grim foretelling for the whole field of robotics. If we cannot reuse the internet and leverage the collective human experience already accumulated, we are cooked. Collecting the entirety of human experience on hardware again via tele-operators will take many years, if not decades, and this would mean physical AI will scale far too slowly. Breakthroughs that allow us to use human data, YouTube, RL models like Gemini and Veo - that scale capabilities as a function of FLOPs and data tokens (even if not from robots) - will then become very valuable to break us out of a slow scaling paradigm."

Great read! I couldn't agree more with the goal-driven mindset instead of picking labels (e.g., what is a VLA, world model, etc.). It's always intriguing to think about what if robot data is no longer a constraint, and it's exciting to see how it's being pushed forward by @peteflorence, @andyzengineer, and folks at @GeneralistAI.

To me, the most intriguing question is not whether scaling data just 'solves robotics,' but that it enables us to ask very different questions and enhance our collective understanding of the shared challenge.

@peteflorence: "..."

If anyone is creating a benchmark for frontier physical AI models, this task is a great one to add to the roster. Sensorimotor end-to-end policies must exhibit long-term visual memory to track and reason about where an object might be.

It’s also harder to cheat on this task, as it can be difficult if there are gaps in the model’s memory, such as low-frame rate memory or coarse representations like language. GEN-1 nails it on the first try with an unseen object, and @BerkayAntmen was really trying hard to fool the model here. Shout out to an excellent task from @RhodaAI.

@GeneralistAI: "GEN-1 plays the shell game, trained on just one hour of robot data. It also generalizes to unseen objects, like @BerkayAntmen's car keys. Physical AI models should be capable of benchmark tasks like this one. It's interesting for all the reasons @RhodaAI calls out — it requires visual memory, and the model must track the cups from the very start at high frame rates. Interestingly, GEN-1 appears to exhibit a degree of 'active perception.' It's subtle; the hands can sometimes appear to 'follow' the cups, using its own movements to help attend to where it thinks the object should be.'"

